<ArticleLayout
  title="Traceable: What It Means to Us"
  description="Orivia's principle of traceability, from Tracr and the Kimberley Process to what science and technology studies says about the limits of the technology."
  backLabel="Home"
  backHref="/#principles"
>
  <p>
    Traceability is the ability to answer, with evidence, where a
    specific parcel of material came from and who handled it before it
    reached its current owner. In gold and diamonds, that answer has
    become a commercial necessity: major buyers, refiners and
    regulators increasingly require it, alongside the ethical case for
    getting it right.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the industry already is</h2>
  <p>
    The diamond trade has the longest institutional history here. The
    Kimberley Process, established in the early 2000s, created a
    government-backed certification scheme to keep conflict diamonds out
    of formal markets, though it has been widely criticised for a narrow
    definition of &ldquo;conflict&rdquo; that leaves other abuses
    unaddressed. More recently, De Beers built Tracr, a blockchain
    platform that follows individual diamonds from mine to retail,
    demonstrating what a full digital chain of custody can look like at
    industrial scale. In gold, the LBMA&rsquo;s Responsible Gold Guidance
    and the Responsible Minerals Initiative set the due diligence
    standards that refiners and downstream buyers are increasingly
    expected to meet.
  </p>

  <h2>What the technology can and cannot prove</h2>
  <p>
    Science and technology studies, the academic field that examines how
    technologies actually function inside social and institutional
    systems, offers a useful caution here: a ledger is only as honest as
    the data entered into it. Blockchain guarantees that a record has not
    been altered after the fact. It says nothing about whether the first
    entry, the claim that this parcel came from this site, was accurate.
    That first-mile verification is a human and procedural problem, not
    a cryptographic one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters especially in ASM, where an estimated 45 million people
    work directly across some 80 countries (World Bank and Pact&rsquo;s
    2023 State of the ASM Sector report), and where artisanal output is
    estimated at roughly 15 to 20 percent of global gold production and
    a similar share of global diamond production. Material from that
    scale of activity enters the same markets as large-scale production,
    frequently through several intermediaries before it is recorded
    anywhere at all.
  </p>

  <h2>How this plays out at Orivia</h2>
  <p>
    We treat traceability as an operating discipline first and a
    technology second. Site-level verification, physical inspection,
    production reconciliation, direct relationships with the people
    running an operation, is what makes the first entry in any ledger
    trustworthy. The blockchain layer then preserves and shares what has
    already been properly verified, for our own operation and for the
    buyers, traders and investors we advise.
  </p>
